{"cards":[{"id":"f4fb7a8d-ae67-4e34-b83c-f2235c72f0d3","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which characteristic is unique to living organisms and distinguishes them from non-living things?","back":"Ability to respond to stimuli\n\nThis question tests the ability to differentiate between characteristics of living organisms. While growth, movement, and reproduction are common to many living organisms, the ability to respond to stimuli is a fundamental characteristic that distinguishes living organisms from non-living things. All living organisms can respond to their environment in some way.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.067Z"},{"id":"7ba2b1c0-dfd2-4ff9-a930-e403a0ab4f80","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which classification system is based on evolutionary relationships and genetic similarities?","back":"Cladistics\n\nThis question tests knowledge of biological classification systems. The cladistics system is based on evolutionary relationships and genetic similarities, grouping organisms based on common ancestors. The Linnaean system is based on physical characteristics, the five-kingdom system categorizes organisms into broad groups, and binomial nomenclature is the system of naming species with a two-part name.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.070Z"},{"id":"8344ad9a-0624-4494-87e9-755d7530cff6","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which of the following is a characteristic of all living organisms?","back":"Reproduction\n\nThis question tests the understanding of the fundamental characteristics of living organisms. While photosynthesis, respiration, and homeostasis are important processes in many living organisms, reproduction is a universal characteristic of all living organisms, as it is essential for the continuation of a species.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.072Z"},{"id":"d3962951-7836-40e2-82b8-8f5adc496b5b","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which of the following is a primary function of carbohydrates in living organisms?","back":"Energy storage\n\nThis question tests the understanding of the primary functions of carbohydrates. Carbohydrates are primarily used for energy storage in living organisms, such as glycogen in animals and starch in plants. Option B is incorrect because structural support is a function of proteins and lipids. Option C is incorrect because catalysis of reactions is a function of enzymes, which are proteins. Option D is incorrect because genetic information storage is a function of nucleic acids, not carbohydrates.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.074Z"},{"id":"703773d8-1f7c-424b-a119-761c0687f861","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which characteristic is unique to living organisms?","back":"Ability to respond to stimuli\n\nThis question tests the understanding of the characteristics unique to living organisms. While all living organisms can grow, move, and reproduce, the ability to respond to stimuli is unique to living organisms. Option A is incorrect because non-living things can also grow in size. Option B is incorrect because machines can move without being alive. Option C is incorrect because some non-living entities can reproduce in a limited sense, such as crystals growing from a solution.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.075Z"},{"id":"a0a432f3-9ff1-4583-8f1d-7f6a2494542a","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which classification system is based on evolutionary relationships?","back":"Cladistics\n\nThis question tests the understanding of different classification systems. Cladistics is a classification system based on evolutionary relationships, grouping organisms based on shared ancestry. Option A is incorrect because the Linnaean system is based on physical characteristics. Option B is incorrect because the five-kingdom system is based on broad categories of organisms. Option D is incorrect because binomial nomenclature is a naming system, not a classification system.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.077Z"},{"id":"ae76e1f8-db4d-44d6-aa73-840221d7ed16","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which characteristic is essential for an organism to be classified as a living organism?","back":"Ability to reproduce\n\nThis question tests the understanding of the essential characteristics of living organisms. The ability to reproduce is a fundamental characteristic of all living organisms, allowing for the continuation of species. Option A is incorrect because not all living organisms can move. Option C is incorrect as not all organisms can photosynthesize. Option D is incorrect because while growth is a characteristic of living organisms, it is not the only essential characteristic.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.078Z"},{"id":"5741e1b4-d615-4d3e-9c69-cd693f8396e6","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which type of biological molecule is primarily responsible for the genetic information in living organisms?","back":"Nucleic acids\n\nThis question tests the understanding of the role of different biological molecules. Nucleic acids, such as DNA and RNA, are responsible for storing and transmitting genetic information. Option A is incorrect because proteins are involved in various functions but not primarily in genetic information storage. Option B is incorrect as lipids are involved in energy storage and membrane structure. Option C is incorrect because carbohydrates are involved in energy storage and structural roles.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.080Z"},{"id":"68b31c88-cddd-425a-819b-652ccee715da","deck_id":"03ea9cb1-ea8f-481c-81e3-a6d7314c8ac1","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which biological molecule is essential for the structure and function of cell membranes?","back":"Lipids\n\nThis question tests the understanding of the role of lipids in cell membranes. Lipids, particularly phospholipids, are crucial for the structure and function of cell membranes, providing a barrier and aiding in cell signaling. Option A is incorrect because proteins are involved in various functions but are not the primary structural component. Option C is incorrect because carbohydrates are mainly involved in energy storage and cell recognition. Option D is incorrect because nucleic acids store genetic information.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","ease_factor":"2.50","interval_days":0,"repetitions":0,"due_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:05.998Z","last_reviewed_at":null,"created_at":"2026-07-09T11:28:06.082Z"},{"id":"042b32fd-d0cb-4609-b211-a84ce0321fb3","deck_id":"beadc521-c6ef-49b0-b182-3b204002f186","user_id":"a14a382f-07f4-4c48-88d2-7d89d4c71606","front":"Which characteristic is unique to living organisms and distinguishes them from non-living things?","back":"Ability to respond to stimuli\n\nThis question tests the ability to differentiate between characteristics of living organisms. While growth, movement, and reproduction are common to many living organisms, the ability to respond to stimuli is a fundamental characteristic that distinguishes living organisms from non-living things. All living organisms can respond to their environment in some way.","front_image_url":null,"front_diagram_svg":null,"hint":"Cambridge-IGCSE-Biology-MARRY-JONES-4th-Ed","subject":